cartridge "ICE TITANS/C.M.BLAKEMORE/1988"

' ICE TITANS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Ice Titans" by Cleveland M. Blakemore,
' Ahoy! January 1988 (Commodore 64 machine language, ICE TITANS.ML at
' $C000, started by a one-line BASIC loader: LOAD then SYS 49152). Made
' for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/icetitans-ml.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a glacier cave under jagged ice crags, an ice cliff with
' four cave mouths, two laser cannons on rails, horned ice titans with
' tridents in three kinds, a frost burst, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: one joystick drives two laser cannons at once. Left and right
' slide the floor cannon (it fires up); up and down slide the side cannon
' (it fires left). Hold a button and both fire. The ice titans come out
' of the caves on the left one at a time and roam the cave on diagonals,
' bouncing off its walls and off one another. A titan that reaches a
' cannon smashes into it: the cannon loses one of its three lives (its
' colour shows them: white, grey, red) and the titan bursts. A cannon with
' no lives left is gone; the game goes on with the other one until both
' are lost.
'
' From the original (orig/): two cannons on one stick (floor cannon left/
' right only, side cannon up/down only, C867 masks $44 and $11), both
' firing on the held button whenever their beam is not already in flight;
' four titans on screen, each coming out of its own cave at the left edge
' (C85F rows), one at a time, walking straight out and then roaming on a
' random diagonal (C82E: directions 2, 4, 6, 8), bouncing off the cave's
' sides (C83F-C857 bounds); a wave ends when 12 + 4 x wave titans have
' been destroyed (02AF), shot or crashed into a cannon (both count and
' both score: C3B2-C3D8); (wave + 1) x 10 points a titan (C880 with X =
' wave + 1 on the tens digit) and 1000 for a cleared wave (X = 100); the
' titans' speed rises with the wave (C836: 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5) and
' their look changes (C86A/C873: three shapes in turns, new colours) up
' to wave 9, which repeats; three lives per cannon shown by its colour
' (C87C); a jingle between waves; six-digit score and high score.
'
' Liberties: the field is the ColecoVision's (titans walk out from caves
' inside the visible area, the C64's from the left border); speeds are
' worked out for a human player at 60 Hz: the titans move 0.75 to 1.75
' pixels a frame (the C64 1 to 5 of its pixels), the cannons 2, the
' beams 5; titans that bump turn away from each other (the C64 picked a
' new random diagonal on every frame they touched); every so often one
' titan charges at the spot where a cannon stands (more often in later
' waves; moving away dodges it): on the C64 a
' cannon could stand still with the button held and let the wandering
' titans walk into its beam; a short gap between two titans coming out;
' collisions are box tests (the C64 used its sprite collision register);
' the last life's colour is red (the C64 went white, grey, dark grey);
' sound effects are new (the C64 had a laser buzz and a titan's death
' cry); GAME OVER then the title (the C64 went straight back to PRESS
' FIRE TO BEGIN).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the cannons, a button
' fires. A button starts a game from the title.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers (the test bot replays them).
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs 25 (sprite count) removed 2026-11-01.

